. The defense has done most of the work, ranking 6th in EPA allowed while the offense sits 29th in EPA per play. That record is better than the underlying numbers, which suggests a few close games have gone their way. What sets them apart is the 2nd highest play-action rate and the highest blitz rate in the league.","offense":"The Vikings offense has struggled, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Schematically, they stand out for the 2nd highest play-action rate in the league. Kyler Murray has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Justin Jefferson, who has drawn 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Vikings have one of the best defenses in the league this season, ranking 7th or better in four of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area has been sack rate, where it leads the league, and it has no real weak spot. Dallas Turner has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Blake Cashman, who has 4.5 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. In terms of scheme, they have the highest blitz rate in the league.","special":"The Vikings have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 86% of field goals, which is about average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the offensive line, about 31% of the money on the books. Their running backs were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Kyler Murray has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,022 yards. Justin Jefferson has been the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 553 receiving yards. Aaron Jones has handled most of the work in the backfield with 61% of the carries. On defense, Andrew Van Ginkel leads the team with 8.5 sacks and Byron Murphy has picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Kevin O'Connell is calling the offense as head coach and Brian Flores is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2024 Broncos under Sean Payton and the 2022 Packers under Matt LaFleur. Their defense looks most like the 2023 and 2022 Giants.","trends":"The offense went from 28th in EPA per play in 2025 to 29th so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 4th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 6th so far this season, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was fourth down go rate, which went from 30% to 39%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}